WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.069 --> 00:00:07.469
So Shiny for Python,
if you're not already

00:00:07.469 --> 00:00:10.949
familiar with Shiny,
Shiny is a framework

00:00:10.949 --> 00:00:15.519
for building interactive
web applications.

00:00:15.519 --> 00:00:18.599
So it's been almost
10 years to the date

00:00:18.599 --> 00:00:23.199
that we've had Shiny in R,
but over the past year or so.

00:00:23.199 --> 00:00:26.159
We've been working on
essentially porting

00:00:26.159 --> 00:00:31.229
that foundation over to Python
so that Python users can

00:00:31.229 --> 00:00:35.219
have a similar, very
approachable framework

00:00:35.219 --> 00:00:39.539
for building interactive web
applications without needing

00:00:39.539 --> 00:00:42.779
to already know HTML,
CSS, and JavaScript.

00:00:42.779 --> 00:00:45.059
So even though we try to
make this approachable,

00:00:45.059 --> 00:00:47.789
we still sort of build
this on a foundation that's

00:00:47.789 --> 00:00:50.799
very flexible and scalable.

00:00:50.799 --> 00:00:55.199
So these are kind of
built on foundations

00:00:55.199 --> 00:01:00.448
that allow you to build out
your own custom components,

00:01:00.448 --> 00:01:03.869
sort extend some of
the reactivity concepts

00:01:03.869 --> 00:01:09.539
to implement your own sort
of modular functionalities.

00:01:09.539 --> 00:01:15.809
And also the sort of reactive
framework that powers

00:01:15.809 --> 00:01:17.009
all of this.

00:01:17.009 --> 00:01:19.169
One of the reasons
why it's so nice

00:01:19.169 --> 00:01:24.809
is it kind of allows you to
declare what should happen,

00:01:24.809 --> 00:01:26.759
and then Shiny
handles the details

00:01:26.759 --> 00:01:30.419
of what actual
computations to execute

00:01:30.419 --> 00:01:33.369
when they need to be executed.

00:01:33.369 --> 00:01:37.319
So it kind of allows you to
write this expressive reactive

00:01:37.319 --> 00:01:41.039
code without needing to handle
every specific case of when

00:01:41.039 --> 00:01:43.869
this changes, then also
update this other thing.

00:01:43.869 --> 00:01:47.739
So for a very sort of
hello world example,

00:01:47.739 --> 00:01:50.669
you can see it here
running in the browser.

00:01:50.669 --> 00:01:56.759
And this is how you would start
programming a very basic Shiny

00:01:56.759 --> 00:01:58.869
application.

00:01:58.869 --> 00:02:02.159
So you can see I'm
importing from Shiny.

00:02:02.159 --> 00:02:05.069
There's a UI package
inside of Shiny.

00:02:05.069 --> 00:02:09.899
So this is how you'll
actually create or define

00:02:09.899 --> 00:02:13.259
where things should go in
the actual user interface

00:02:13.259 --> 00:02:16.109
of the Shiny application.

00:02:16.109 --> 00:02:17.759
There's also a
rendering package.

00:02:17.759 --> 00:02:23.009
And this is sort of
utilities for rendering

00:02:23.009 --> 00:02:27.989
things like matplotlib plots
or various different types

00:02:27.989 --> 00:02:31.439
of Python output into
actual stuff that

00:02:31.439 --> 00:02:34.379
can go into a user interface.

00:02:34.379 --> 00:02:36.209
And then every
Shiny application is

00:02:36.209 --> 00:02:40.589
going to want this capital
A, App, to actually bring

00:02:40.589 --> 00:02:43.869
the two main pieces of
the Shiny app together.

00:02:43.869 --> 00:02:48.489
That being the user interface
here from lines 3 to 6.

00:02:48.489 --> 00:02:53.099
This is the definition of what
you see on the right here,

00:02:53.099 --> 00:02:55.499
as well as the server
logic, which kind of

00:02:55.499 --> 00:03:01.649
takes Python objects and
renders them into things that

00:03:01.649 --> 00:03:05.439
can go into the user interface.

00:03:05.439 --> 00:03:09.009
So in this particular
basic example,

00:03:09.009 --> 00:03:12.299
I have created
this user interface

00:03:12.299 --> 00:03:16.899
by first creating sort
of a page container.

00:03:16.899 --> 00:03:20.609
So we have several different
variants on different types

00:03:20.609 --> 00:03:22.229
of pages that you can create.

00:03:22.229 --> 00:03:26.399
Page fluid in a lot of
cases is it's pretty basic,

00:03:26.399 --> 00:03:30.869
but covers most use cases
for just starting a new user

00:03:30.869 --> 00:03:33.149
interface.

00:03:33.149 --> 00:03:37.949
And then inside of there, I
will put a slider component,

00:03:37.949 --> 00:03:41.759
and give this an
ID of lowercase N.

00:03:41.759 --> 00:03:44.369
And then this is a label
that should appear just

00:03:44.369 --> 00:03:45.789
above the slider.

00:03:45.789 --> 00:03:49.829
So I'm going to put a
capital N for a label.

00:03:49.829 --> 00:03:52.439
Define the minimum and the
maximum for the slider,

00:03:52.439 --> 00:03:54.479
and then a starting
value for that slider.

00:03:54.479 --> 00:03:59.099
And the way that
Shiny works is you,

00:03:59.099 --> 00:04:01.079
basically in this
user interface,

00:04:01.079 --> 00:04:04.919
you give every kind of user
interface component an ID,

00:04:04.919 --> 00:04:08.609
and then you can
reference whatever

00:04:08.609 --> 00:04:12.059
the current value of that
input is on the server

00:04:12.059 --> 00:04:13.184
by saying input.n.

00:04:15.704 --> 00:04:18.989
And then you kind
of read that value

00:04:18.989 --> 00:04:22.029
by calling it like a function.

00:04:22.029 --> 00:04:25.529
So if I had several
different input components

00:04:25.529 --> 00:04:29.139
with different IDs
I would, of course,

00:04:29.139 --> 00:04:31.109
change the name
here after the dots,

00:04:31.109 --> 00:04:34.109
and then call it like a
function to basically read

00:04:34.109 --> 00:04:35.669
whatever the current value is.

00:04:35.669 --> 00:04:40.139
And you might have noticed
in this server definition

00:04:40.139 --> 00:04:44.279
when you define this
server function,

00:04:44.279 --> 00:04:45.899
it takes three arguments.

00:04:45.899 --> 00:04:50.189
The input values that are coming
in from this user interface.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:55.409
An output argument,
and we're going

00:04:55.409 --> 00:05:03.549
to use this output object
to basically hook up values

00:05:03.549 --> 00:05:08.649
that we create on the server
up with the user interface.

00:05:08.649 --> 00:05:10.719
And then also a
session, which we're not

00:05:10.719 --> 00:05:13.599
going to get into
here, but this can

00:05:13.599 --> 00:05:18.639
allow you to do things specific
to a specific user's session

00:05:18.639 --> 00:05:19.959
experience.

00:05:19.959 --> 00:05:23.859
So with this server
definition, I'm

00:05:23.859 --> 00:05:27.609
going to create an
output that's going

00:05:27.609 --> 00:05:30.969
to be connected to this
output text_verbatim

00:05:30.969 --> 00:05:34.059
with an ID of text.

00:05:34.059 --> 00:05:38.589
And with every output,
when you first create it,

00:05:38.589 --> 00:05:44.769
you're going to want to then
provide a rendering decorator.

00:05:44.769 --> 00:05:49.989
So here @render.text
basically means

00:05:49.989 --> 00:05:51.969
I want to define
a function that's

00:05:51.969 --> 00:05:55.629
going to return a string,
that I can just put verbatim

00:05:55.629 --> 00:06:01.329
into this whatever UI component
I'm hooking up this return

00:06:01.329 --> 00:06:03.159
value to.

00:06:03.159 --> 00:06:06.309
And this part right here,
the actual name of this

00:06:06.309 --> 00:06:08.979
function that I'm providing
to the render text

00:06:08.979 --> 00:06:11.949
and the output
decorator is going

00:06:11.949 --> 00:06:16.129
to define the output
ID essentially

00:06:16.129 --> 00:06:20.169
that should match this
sort of UI container

00:06:20.169 --> 00:06:22.869
that I want to hook
up this value to.

00:06:22.869 --> 00:06:26.319
Now that I've kind of declared
that there's an output that I

00:06:26.319 --> 00:06:30.249
want to render as text
with an ID of text or txt,

00:06:30.249 --> 00:06:33.219
and provide it inside of--

00:06:33.219 --> 00:06:36.099
and provide this sort
of function expression

00:06:36.099 --> 00:06:41.499
that reads a reactive
value, this input.n,

00:06:41.499 --> 00:06:43.749
by declaring it
this way, then Shiny

00:06:43.749 --> 00:06:48.309
knows basically whenever
this inputs and changes

00:06:48.309 --> 00:06:52.479
I will re-execute this
function, and basically

00:06:52.479 --> 00:06:57.609
create a new return value
for this text verbatim here.

00:06:57.609 --> 00:07:02.319
So that's a long way
of saying like whenever

00:07:02.319 --> 00:07:07.329
I change this slider value
here, this gray text box below

00:07:07.329 --> 00:07:10.839
is going to update to be 2 times
whatever the value this slider

00:07:10.839 --> 00:07:11.469
is.

00:07:11.469 --> 00:07:13.539
A neat thing about this
that I don't really

00:07:13.539 --> 00:07:17.019
want to steal my colleague
Winston's thunder too

00:07:17.019 --> 00:07:22.009
much on is the fact that this
is all running in the browser.

00:07:22.009 --> 00:07:24.939
So we've kind of
built this foundation

00:07:24.939 --> 00:07:30.549
for allowing you to take
simple Shiny applications

00:07:30.549 --> 00:07:33.699
and run them purely in the
browser without a Python

00:07:33.699 --> 00:07:35.869
process running
in the background.

00:07:35.869 --> 00:07:39.339
So this is actually
statically hosted on Netlify

00:07:39.339 --> 00:07:41.259
through GitHub pages.

00:07:41.259 --> 00:07:43.359
And I have a running
Shiny application

00:07:43.359 --> 00:07:48.879
here that I can actually modify
the source code of this Shiny

00:07:48.879 --> 00:07:52.839
application and say I want maybe
five times the current value

00:07:52.839 --> 00:07:54.699
of this slider.

00:07:54.699 --> 00:07:58.779
Restart, it's going to start
with an initial value of 40

00:07:58.779 --> 00:07:59.529
here.

00:07:59.529 --> 00:08:03.289
And now I've got 5
times 40 in the output.

00:08:03.289 --> 00:08:05.319
So that's just kind
of a neat side note

00:08:05.319 --> 00:08:08.109
that you'll notice as I'm kind
of going through more examples.

00:08:08.109 --> 00:08:12.229
Some of these will just
run purely in the browser.

00:08:12.229 --> 00:08:14.799
But you can learn more
about that in another video.

00:08:14.799 --> 00:08:19.509
Currently, I don't know if this
link will change at any point.

00:08:19.509 --> 00:08:22.299
I would imagine that
it will eventually

00:08:22.299 --> 00:08:27.279
have the company's branding and
everything in the link name.

00:08:27.279 --> 00:08:30.638
But for now, yes,
pyshiny.netlify.app

00:08:30.638 --> 00:08:32.559
is the best place to go to.

00:08:32.559 --> 00:08:35.169
And then there's
some getting started

00:08:35.169 --> 00:08:40.479
stuff, some information on
how to install information

00:08:40.479 --> 00:08:43.179
about how to deploy to
our different professional

00:08:43.179 --> 00:08:47.709
products, going into
some concepts in depth,

00:08:47.709 --> 00:08:53.019
and then there's a link
here to examples that

00:08:53.019 --> 00:08:56.979
is kind of a different site with
all sorts of different examples

00:08:56.979 --> 00:08:59.169
that are running in the browser.

00:08:59.169 --> 00:09:02.519
[MUSIC PLAYING]

